// Create a Texture Object and assign it parameters and storage
glGenTextures(&handle)
glBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, handle) // Tell him all future calls will set the 2D Texture bound to it 
  glTexParameter(GL_TEXTURE_2D, ...);
  glGenerateMipmap(GL_TEXTURE_2D); // Driver/GPU will generate the mipmaps for you
  glTexImage2D(...); 
glBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, 0);


// In the Rendering Loop
glActiveTexture(texture_uint_number); // Essentially you are saying the next bind will bind that texture to this on chip Texture Unit
glBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, handle);
glUniform1iARB(glsl_sampler_variable_handle, texture_unit_from_active_texture); 
					     // This will assign that Sampler2D variable in your glsl Shader to the given Texture
					     // Object and the Texture Unit. That's it, Texture2D calls from inside the shader
					     // will now get the state from this Texture and go through that HW unit and use 
					     // the mentioned Sampler parameters
					     // Or read this for more details
					     // http://www.opengl.org/wiki/GLSL_Sampler#Binding_textures_to_samplers


--------- Compression formats -----
For desktop you GL_EXT_texture_compression_s3tc which supports DXT1/3/5 modes
There is also RGTC and BPTC

The former is ubiquitous. Put your textures into .dds formats and load them directly in the compressed format into GL with glCompressedTexImage2D

Also for MESA you need to install S3TC support separately. Look at http://stefanjones.ca/blog/quickstart-for-radeonsi-driver-and-steam-on-ubuntu-14-04/
or apt search dxtn

